<!-- Nomad vs Yesim Comparison Content -->
<div class="content-section">

  {% set nomadBest = providerA.codes | findCode("TRAVELESIMEXPERT") %}
  {% set nomadBundle = providerA.codes | findCode("REG3") %}
  {% set yesimPrimary = providerB.codes | findCode(providerB.primaryCode) %}

  <div class="content-callout">
    <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
    <p>
      Pick <strong>Nomad</strong> if you're abroad for months and want one prepaid bundle that stays valid for up to a year. Pick <strong>Yesim</strong> if you fly often and would rather keep one dollar balance that rolls over between trips, or if you want the option of a refund on a plan you never used. Neither is the cheap choice for a single short trip, so check <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> first if price per GB is all you care about.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The Core Difference: A Fixed Bundle or a Rolling Balance</h2>
  <p>
    Both are app-based eSIMs aimed at people who travel more than once, but they solve it in opposite ways. Nomad's <strong>Global-EX</strong> is a bundle you buy once: on Nomad's own plan page that's 10GB for 180 days at $39, or 20GB, 30GB, or 50GB valid for 365 days at $59, $79, and $129, covering 98 countries. Nothing renews, and the 50GB bundle works out to $2.58/GB.
  </p>
  <p>
    Yesim's <strong>Pay &amp; Fly</strong> is closer to a prepaid balance. You buy data in dollars rather than gigabytes, it's priced from $1.10/GB, and whatever you don't use rolls over to your next trip instead of expiring when this one ends. The catch is that Yesim's ordinary day-based plans sit in our "Expensive" bracket, roughly $1.56 to $31 depending on destination.
  </p>

  <h2>Snapshot</h2>
  <div class="content-box">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th></th>
          <th>Nomad</th>
          <th>Yesim</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Long-stay option</td><td>Global-EX: 10GB/180 days $39, up to 50GB/365 days $129</td><td>Pay &amp; Fly balance from $1.10/GB, unused balance carries over</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Refund</td><td>Not covered in this comparison, check Nomad's policy</td><td>30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans, advertised by Yesim</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Try before buying</td><td>Free 1GB trial eSIM for new customers</td><td>None that we've seen</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Support</td><td>24/7 live chat and email</td><td>See Yesim's help center</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Network</td><td>Local partner networks per country</td><td>Advertises automatic switching across 800+ partner networks (Yesim's claim, not measured by us)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="content-note">Figures come from each provider's own site and our <a href="/nomad/">Nomad</a> and <a href="/yesim/">Yesim</a> pages. Confirm the live price at checkout.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Discount Codes</h2>
  <div class="content-box content-box--tan">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Nomad:</strong> TRAVELESIMEXPERT, {{ nomadBest.discount | lower }}, or REG3, {{ nomadBundle.discount | lower }}. <a href="/nomad/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
      <li><strong>Yesim:</strong> {{ providerB.primaryCode }}, {{ yesimPrimary.discount | lower }}. First purchase only. <a href="/yesim/">Full code list &rarr;</a></li>
    </ul>
    <p class="content-note">Neither code is ours. These are public codes shown on each provider's own site or promo page. TRAVELESIMEXPERT only works on Nomad's Global plans, and REG3 is a flat $3 USD off, so it matters most on small plans.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Is Nomad or Yesim the Right Pick</h2>
  <div class="decision-grid">
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--primary">
      <h3>Go with Nomad if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You'll be away for months and want one prepaid bundle valid for up to 365 days</li>
        <li>You want to test the service first with the free 1GB trial eSIM</li>
        <li>You want 24/7 live chat support</li>
        <li>You want a Nomad Global plan and can use TRAVELESIMEXPERT</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="decision-card decision-card--secondary">
      <h3>Go with Yesim if&hellip;</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You fly often and want unused data to carry over to the next trip</li>
        <li>You buy ahead and want a 30-day money-back option on a plan you never used</li>
        <li>You'd rather pay in dollars for data used than track a fixed GB allowance</li>
        <li>You want an alternative to a single-carrier eSIM and are happy to rely on Yesim's network-switching claim</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two Things Worth Knowing Before You Decide</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Yesim's first-purchase code is the biggest of the four.</strong> TEE20 gives 20% off, but only on your first order. After that, Yesim's other public codes are smaller: WELCOMETOYESIM at 15% and TRAVELFREE at a flat &euro;3 off. Nomad's TRAVELESIMEXPERT isn't limited to a first order, but it only applies to Global plans.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Neither is built for one cheap week away.</strong> If you only need data for a single short trip, <a href="/airhub/">Airhub</a> or <a href="/holafly/">Holafly</a> are the first places to look. If you'd rather buy data for one country now and use it with no deadline, <a href="/bnesim/">BNEsim</a> sells no-expiration packs. For device setup, see our <a href="/compatible-devices/">eSIM compatible devices</a> guide.
  </p>

  <h2>Nomad vs Yesim FAQ</h2>
  <div class="content-faq">
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which is better for months abroad</h3>
      <p>Nomad, if you want a single purchase. Its Global-EX bundles run from 10GB for 180 days to 50GB for 365 days across 98 countries. Yesim's Pay &amp; Fly balance suits repeat trips better, since unused balance carries over.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Does Yesim offer a refund</h3>
      <p>Yesim advertises a 30-day money-back guarantee on unused plans, which goes further than providers that only refund an eSIM that never connected. Refunds run through Yesim's own policy, not through us.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Can I try either one for free</h3>
      <p>Nomad offers new customers a free 1GB trial eSIM. We haven't seen an equivalent from Yesim.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="content-faq-item">
      <h3>Which discount code is bigger</h3>
      <p>Nomad's TRAVELESIMEXPERT is 20% off Global plans only, and REG3 is $3 USD off any eSIM. Yesim's TEE20 is 20% off your first purchase only.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

